From 9c8138113d1c1110a35424e9553ab5efa74f6a95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JohannSSK <321048248+JohannSSK@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 14:34:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Require a session on every team-member route
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every route under /api/admin/employee/ trusted Parent_id and Employee_id
alone — two guessable integers — with nothing proving who was calling. An
unauthenticated curl could spend the company's API key, read another
member's chat history, delete their chats, switch the policy gates off, or
send a blocked prompt by adding bypass: true.

- New src/auth.py: team-member sessions, mirroring the existing
  administrator sessions (SHA-256 hash stored, 7-day expiry, deleted on
  use once expired). require_member() authorises a member route, and is
  the single place the rule now lives.
- src/app.py: issue a token at member login, revoke it when the member is
  removed, require it on chat and settings, and create the table at
  startup.
- src/chat_store.py, src/models_route.py: require it on chat history
  (all four routes), and on model discovery, which makes an outbound call
  on the caller's behalf.
- Overrides are now authorised server-side against the administrator's
  own session token, before anything is forwarded. The browser still
  collects the approval; it no longer gets to grant it.
- src/team_stats.py: an approved override stays in the audit trail but is
  no longer counted as a block, since the message was in fact sent.
- src/static/js/main.js: send the member's session token, send the
  administrator's token separately for an override, and stop dropping the
  per-request check flags on the way to the server.
- tools/test_auth.py: 21 regression tests. They found a route that had
  been missed, which is the point of having them.

Existing sessions are not carried over: a member signed in before this
change signs in again once.

Co-authored-by: arena-agent <297053741+arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
